[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "ayatori-coscientist-iteration-06", :doc/title "Co-Scientist iteration 06 - packing the novelty window turns the chain walk's 2x loss into a 1.91x win, and the boundary is where the rule said", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-06.edn", :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 06 - the read-locality pack, built and measured\n\n> ⚠ **CORRECTED by iteration 07 (2026-09-09).** The 1.91x below is a 64-link\n> chain. kotobase's novelty chain is **4 links** at the fold threshold\n> (`novelty-segment-size` = 16, measured in kotobase-peer\n> `novelty_chain_depth_test`), and at 4 links the packed arm's fixed 3 per window\n> does not amortise: window packing then saves **one round trip** (7 vs 8), and\n> at 1–2 links it LOSES up to two. Everything measured here is reproducible and\n> correct for the chain it walks; the error is in reading it as production.\n\n\n> Seed from iteration 05: \"Build the read-locality pack: seal one along the\n> novelty window rather than along a commit, and re-run E. The prediction is\n> N/P = 64 and it is now a prediction with a number in it.\"\n\nBuilt. `bench/novelty_window.cljk`. The prediction was written into the file\nheader BEFORE running it, as a closed form rather than a single number, so that\nwhat came back could disagree at seven points instead of one.\n\n    ratio = 2W / (W + 3)      per window: 2 (open) + W (blocks) + 1 (catalog)\n                              against per-object's 2W\n\n## G. Measured, 64 commits, all history sealed\n\n    W   packs | per-object | packed  catalog  reads  opened    N/P      vs  predicted\n    1      64         128      256       64    192      64   3.00   0.50x      0.50x\n    2      32         128      160       32    128      32   4.00   0.80x      0.80x\n    3      22         128      130       22    108      22   4.91   0.98x      1.00x\n    4      16         128      112       16     96      16   6.00   1.14x      1.14x\n    8       8         128       88        8     80       8  10.00   1.45x      1.45x\n   16       4         128       76        4     72       4  18.00   1.68x      1.68x\n   64       1         128       67        1     66       1  66.00   1.91x      1.91x\n\n**W = 1 reproduces iteration 05's 0.50x exactly**, at 64 commits rather than the\n2-16 it used. That is this file's control: W = 1 IS the commit-scoped policy, so\na harness that could not reproduce the loss would be measuring itself.\n\n**W = 64 turns that 2x loss into a 1.91x win** - a 3.8x swing from changing only\nthe grouping, with the mechanism, the codec, the catalog and the walk untouched.\nThe chain walk was the one access path where ADR-2608160100's own justification\nwas contradicted by measurement; it no longer is, under window grouping.\n\n### The boundary is where iteration 02's rule said it would be\n\nIteration 02 put the crossover at N/P > 3 cold. Window packing makes N/P = W, so\nthe rule predicts its own boundary at W = 3, and W = 2, 3, 4 were measured for\nexactly that reason - a comparison whose inputs never sit on the line cannot tell\ntwo operators apart.\n\n    W=2  0.80x  loses      W=3  0.98x  a tie      W=4  1.14x  wins\n\nW = 3 came back at 0.98x against a predicted 1.00x, and the 0.02 is not noise:\n64 commits in windows of 3 is 21 full windows plus ONE window holding a single\ncommit, and that last window pays the full W + 3 for one block. The closed form\nassumes every window is full. **The prediction was right and the arithmetic that\nproduced it was slightly wrong, which is only visible because the boundary was\nmeasured instead of reasoned about.**\n\n## The win is capped at 2x, and iteration 05's phrasing invited misreading\n\nPer-object pays 2 per block (discover + fetch); packed pays 1 plus a fixed 3 per\nwindow. So `2W / (W + 3) -> 2` and **nothing on this access path can beat 2x.**\nW = 64 reaches 1.91x, which is 96% of the ceiling; W = 16 already reaches 84%.\n\nIteration 05 wrote that N/P = 64 is \"an order of magnitude above either\ncrossover\". True of N/P, and it is not a speedup - the speedup is 1.91x. Recorded\nhere because the two numbers appear one line apart in that file and the larger\none is the one that gets quoted.\n\nAlso: the per-object arm CANNOT be warmed out of its 2, unlike iteration 05\nsection D where warming one arm produced a meaningless 6.00x. A chain visits each\nCID exactly once, so a provider cache never gets a second look at anything. This\nis the one shape where the cold comparison is the only comparison.\n\n## H. The live tail, which a window has and a commit does not\n\nA commit-scoped pack can be sealed the moment the commit lands. A window cannot:\nit is sealed when it CLOSES. So up to W - 1 of the newest commits have no pack,\nand a read of the live head walks per-object until it reaches sealed history -\nhitting the unsealed ones FIRST, because they are the newest.\n\nMeasured at W = 8, mixed arm = sealed packs + per-object for the tail:\n\n    commits  sealed  tail | per-object | mixed  catalog  reads  po-hits     vs\n          8       8     0           16      11        1     10        0  1.45x\n         12       8     4           24      19        1     10        8  1.26x\n         16      16     0           32      22        2     20        0  1.45x\n         20      16     4           40      30        2     20        8  1.33x\n         24      24     0           48      33        3     30        0  1.45x\n\n**The tail dilutes the win; it does not reverse it.** 1.45x with the window\nclosed, 1.26x-1.33x with 4 of the newest commits unsealed. A live head therefore\nreads somewhere between those two figures at all times, oscillating as windows\nclose, and the floor is the diluted number rather than the clean one.\n\nThat is the cost the ADR's grouping does not pay, and it is the honest price of\nthis change. It is also bounded and it shrinks as history grows.\n\n## What this means for the cutover\n\nRoot ADR-2608170300's cutover was recorded as blocked, and the block was cleared\n2026-09-07 (measured 2026-09-09, ALL GREEN at ENTITIES = 25). The remaining\nquestion was whether commit-scoped packing is the right thing to cut over TO, and\niteration 05's 0.50x said no.\n\n**It is now a design change with a measured before and after, not an objection.**\nThe grouping to declare is the novelty window, and root ADR-2608021000 supplies\nthe width: default-fold-threshold 64, described as the most novelty a healthy\ngraph carries between folds. At W = 64 this measures 1.91x on the access path\nthat ADR calls 97% of hydrate's sequential term.\n\nUnchanged and still unmeasured: the original cutover gate - `:packed-write` /\n`:packed-reads` from env.testnet and `:fallback-lookups` reaching zero.\n\n## A defect found by reproducing the harness\n\n`bin/classpath.txt` did not carry `.deps/text/src`, and every bench and test file\nin this repo requires `kotoba.lang.text` - so the reproduction command printed in\neach bench's own header failed with `Could not find namespace: kotoba.lang.text`\nbefore a single number was produced. `deps.edn` pins the repo (as an explicit\nfloor, with a comment explaining why); the hand-maintained classpath and the\nREADME's clone list both omitted it.\n\nMeasured both directions before fixing: with `.deps/text/src` the suite is 141\ntests / 421 assertions / 0 failures and every bench runs; without it, every one\nof them dies at load. Fixed in this change.\n\n## Seeds for iteration 07\n\n  - Everything here is one host and an in-memory range server. The next honest\n    step is a real HTTP Range server, where an open is a connection and the 2\n    reads it costs may not be 2 round trips.\n  - Window packing needs a SEALER: something that watches the novelty window and\n    seals at the fold threshold. Nothing writes one. Its failure modes are the\n    interesting part - a window that never closes because commits stopped, and a\n    fold that lands mid-window.\n  - Measure a query (not a chain) under window grouping. Iterations 02-05 put\n    narrow index-shaped queries at 0.71x-1.00x under commit grouping; the window\n    changes what shares a pack, so that figure should be re-taken rather than\n    assumed to move the same way."}]
